Python Tuples & Sets
What if you want to store data that shouldn't change? What if you want to eliminate duplicate values automatically?
Python provides two data structures for these situations:
•	Tuples: Ordered, immutable collections.
•	Sets: Unordered collections of unique elements.
1. What is a Tuple?
A tuple is an ordered, immutable collection of elements.
•	Lists use square brackets [].
•	Tuples generally use parentheses ().
Property	Tuple
Ordered	Yes
Mutable	No
Allows duplicates	Yes
Allows different data types	Yes
Supports indexing	Yes
Supports slicing	Yes
Why do tuples exist?
Imagine storing a student's date of birth.
dob = (15, 8, 2007)
You generally don't want this information accidentally modified throughout your program.
A tuple communicates that the collection is intended to remain unchanged.
Developer insight: Immutability helps make data handling more predictable. However, remember that a tuple can contain mutable objects, which we'll examine later.
Accessing Tuple Elements
Tuple indexing works exactly like list indexing.
Negative indexing also works.
Element	Index	Negative Index
Python	0	-4
Java	1	-3
C++	2	-2
JavaScript	3	-1
3. Tuple Slicing
Slicing works just like lists.
numbers=(10,20,30,40,50,60)
print(numbers[1:4])
print(numbers[:3])
print(numbers[::-1])
Output:
(20, 30, 40)
(10, 20, 30)
(60, 50, 40, 30, 20, 10)
The slicing syntax remains:
tuple_name[start:end:step]
	
4. Tuple Immutability
This is the most important property of tuples.
Because tuples are immutable.
Once created, their element references cannot be reassigned through tuple indexing.
But can we modify a tuple indirectly?
Consider this:
numbers = (10, 20, 30)numbers = (40, 50, 60)print(numbers)
Output:
(40, 50, 60)
Why did this work?
Because we didn't modify the original tuple.
We created a new tuple and assigned it to the variable numbers.
•	Modifying an existing tuple: Not allowed.
•	Reassigning a variable to another tuple: Allowed.
Method	Purpose
count()	Counts occurrences of an element
index()	Returns the first occurrence's index
Example:
numbers = (10, 20, 30, 20, 40, 20)

print(numbers.count(20))
print(numbers.index(30))
output:
3
2
TUPLE UNPACKING
This is a very useful Python feature.
Consider:
student = ("Pramod", 18, "CSE")
You want to extract each value into a separate variable.
Traditional approach:
name = student[0]
age = student[1]
branch = student[2]
But Python offers a cleaner approach.
Tuple Unpacking
student = ("Pramod", 18, "CSE")
name, age, branch = student
print(name)
print(age)
print(branch)
Output:
Pramod
18
CSE
Python automatically assigns the tuple elements to the corresponding variables.
Important: Number of Variables
This works:
a, b, c = (10, 20, 30)
This raises an error:
a, b = (10, 20, 30)
Why?
Because there are three values but only two variables.
Extended Unpacking
Python also supports the * operator.
numbers = (10, 20, 30, 40, 50)
first, *middle, last = numbers
print(first)
print(middle)
print(last)
Output:
10
[20, 30, 40]
50
Notice something interesting:
middle becomes a list, even though the original collection was a tuple.
SETS
Now let's explore another important data structure.
7. What is a Set?
A set is an unordered collection of unique elements.
Example:
numbers = {10, 20, 30, 40}
print(numbers)
Output:
{10, 20, 30, 40}
Now consider:
numbers = {10, 20, 30, 10, 20, 40}
print(numbers)
Output:
{10, 20, 30, 40}
Notice that duplicate values disappeared.
Sets automatically maintain unique elements.
Characteristics of Sets
Property	Set
Ordered	No
Mutable	Yes
Allows duplicates	No
Supports indexing	No
Supports slicing	No
Allows immutable elements	Yes
Sets are useful for:
•	Removing duplicates.
•	Finding common elements.
•	Comparing collections.
•	Membership testing.
•	Mathematical set operations.

Creating Sets
There are two common approaches.
Using curly braces:
numbers = {10, 20, 30}
Using the set() constructor:
numbers = set([10, 20, 30])
Both create sets.
Important: Empty Sets
Consider:
a = {}
print(type(a))
Output:
<class 'dict'>
Why?
Because {} creates an empty dictionary, not a set.
To create an empty set:
a = set()
print(type(a))
Output:
<class 'set'>
Remember this distinction.

9. Adding Elements
Use add() to insert an element.
numbers = {10, 20, 30} 
numbers.add(40)
print(numbers)
Output:
{10, 20, 30, 40}
Adding an existing element doesn't create a duplicate.
numbers.add(20)
print(numbers)
The set remains unchanged.
Adding Multiple Elements
Use update().
numbers = {10, 20, 30}
numbers.update([40, 50, 60])
print(numbers)
Output:
{10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60}
Method	Behaviour
remove()	Removes an element; raises KeyError if absent
discard()	Removes an element; does nothing if absent
pop()	Removes and returns an arbitrary element
clear()	Removes all elements
MATHEMATICAL SET OPERATIONS
This is where sets become particularly powerful.
Imagine two students have different programming language interests.
student_A = {"Python", "Java", "C++"}
student_B = {"Python", "JavaScript", "C++"}
We want to compare their interests.
Mathematical set operations make this easy.
11. Union
Union combines all unique elements from both sets.
Mathematical notation: A ∪ B
Python:
result = student_A.union(student_B)
print(result)
Output (order may vary):
{'Python', 'Java', 'C++', 'JavaScript'}
You can also use:
result = student_A | student_B
Intersection
Intersection returns elements common to both sets.
Mathematical notation: A ∩ B
Python:
result = student_A.intersection(student_B)
print(result)
Output:
{'Python', 'C++'}
You can also use:
result = student_A & student_B
________________________________________
13. Difference
Difference returns elements present in the first set but absent from the second.
Mathematical notation: A − B
Python:
result = student_A.difference(student_B)
print(result)
Output:
{'Java'}
You can also use:
result = student_A - student_B
Important:
student_A - student_B
is not necessarily equal to:
student_B - student_A
Difference is directional.
Symmetric Difference
This returns elements that exist in either set, but not both.
Mathematical notation: A △ B
Python:
result = student_A.symmetric_difference(student_B)
print(result)
Output:
{'Java', 'JavaScript'}
You can also use:
result = student_A ^ student_B
Quick Comparison
Operation	Meaning	Operator
Union	All unique elements	|
Intersection	Common elements	&
Difference	Elements only in first set	-
Symmetric Difference	Elements in either, but not both	^
FROZEN SETS
15. What is a Frozen Set?
A frozen set is an immutable version of a set.
Example:
numbers = frozenset([10, 20, 30, 40])
print(numbers)
You can perform operations such as:
print(numbers.union({50, 60}))
print(numbers.intersection({20, 30, 50}))
But you cannot add or remove elements from the original frozen set.
numbers.add(50)
This raises an AttributeError.
Set vs Frozen Set
Feature	Set	Frozen Set
Mutable	Yes	No
Duplicates	Not allowed	Not allowed
Union	Yes	Yes
Intersection	Yes	Yes
Add elements	Yes	No
Remove elements	Yes	No
Hashable	No	Yes
Why does hashability matter?
Frozen sets can be used as dictionary keys or as elements of other sets. Regular mutable sets cannot.
WHICH DATA STRUCTURE SHOULD YOU USE?
This is a decision-making skill you'll use throughout your programming career.
Requirement	Best Choice
Store an ordered, changeable collection	List
Store an ordered, immutable collection	Tuple
Store unique elements	Set
Store unique, immutable elements	Frozen Set
Example:
subjects = ["Python", "DBMS", "Python"]
Use a list when duplicates and order matter.
subjects = ("Python", "DBMS", "Python")
Use a tuple when the collection should remain unchanged.
subjects = {"Python", "DBMS"}
Use a set when you only need unique elements.
